Adaptive Causal Resilience Layer (ACRL v2): Mathematical Foundations, Viability Theorems, and Technical Specifications

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Gemini, Deepseek 


Abstract

We introduce ACRL v2, a control paradigm for multi‑agent systems that guarantees resilience against unmodeled disturbances by reconfiguring the system’s causal structure rather than applying additive forces. The plant is modelled by a class of causal stochastic differential equations (C‑SDEs) whose coupling topology is given by a directed acyclic graph G_c. Interventions are structural operations \mathrm{do}(u) that prune edges of G_c, directly altering the infinitesimal generator of the state process. A causal risk functional is defined through the Laplace transform of the first‑passage time to a catastrophic set and is characterised via a Feynman–Kac elliptic boundary‑value problem. To avoid circular dependencies, we construct a policy‑independent causal barrier function from the worst‑case admissible risk. The global viability is encoded in a unified viability functional \mathcal{V} that multiplies the barrier, a meta‑learning stability term (modelled as a log‑submartingale jump‑diffusion), and an exponential metabolic cost. The central result is a Fundamental Theorem of Causal Viability, which establishes almost‑sure invariance of the safe envelope under a unified Hamilton–Jacobi barrier condition applied directly to \ln\mathcal{V}. The proof resolves subtle stochastic drift and jump‑concavity issues, and the framework is extended to account for delays in causal graph identification and to treat the risk functional as a viscosity solution of a Hamilton‑Jacobi–Bellman equation. Finally, we show how the controller can proactively simplify its own causal topology to reduce the graph identification delay \tau_G, creating a self‑aware resilience loop. The paper constitutes a rigorous mathematical research programme at the intersection of stochastic control, causal inference, and viability theory.

Keywords: causal stochastic differential equations, do‑calculus, control barrier functions, viability theory, Feynman–Kac formula, jump‑diffusions, viscosity solutions, causal graph design.

