1. Introduction

Critical autonomous infrastructures—satellite constellations, power networks, drone fleets—must survive shocks whose nature and propagation pathways are unknown a priori. Classical robust control augments the system with safety margins derived from a fixed model. When the disturbance alters the very dependencies among state variables, those margins collapse. ACRL v2 (Adaptive Causal Resilience Layer) proposes a radical shift: the control action is not a force added to the dynamics, but a structural intervention that rewires the causal graph G_c governing the system. By pruning or reorienting causal edges, the system changes its own response to perturbations before physical symptoms become catastrophic.

This paper lays down the rigorous mathematical foundations of ACRL v2. The architecture is built on five pillars:

1. C‑SDEs: The physical plant obeys a stochastic differential equation whose drift and diffusion are parametrised by a directed acyclic graph (DAG) G_c.
2. do‑operator: A control u\in\mathcal{U} maps to a new DAG G'_c(u); the controlled SDE uses G'_c(u) instead of G_c.
3. Causal risk and Feynman–Kac: The probability of catastrophic failure is measured by a discounted first‑passage functional \mathcal{R}_u, which is the unique solution of an elliptic PDE linked to the generator.
4. Policy‑independent barrier and unified viability: A causal barrier function h is built from the worst‑case admissible risk, eliminating circular dependencies. The global viability functional \mathcal{V} = h \cdot V \cdot e^{-C(u)} is introduced, and a unified Hamilton–Jacobi barrier condition is imposed directly on \ln\mathcal{V} to guarantee invariance.
5. Proactive topology design: The controller can explicitly penalise graph complexity, thereby reducing the time \tau_G needed to identify the true causal structure after a disturbance, and tightening the safety margin.

The main result, the Fundamental Theorem of Causal Viability, provides a set of conditions under which the system never enters the failure set \mathcal{F} almost surely. The proof carefully handles three subtle mathematical challenges: (i) the Jensen gap when passing from a sub‑martingale to its logarithm in the presence of jump‑diffusion meta‑learning; (ii) the correct sign and scaling of the barrier drift in the logarithmic representation; and (iii) the treatment of the switched generator as a viscosity solution framework.

The paper is deliberately structured as a mathematical research programme: all hypotheses are stated precisely, the proofs are developed to the greatest extent currently possible, and the remaining open problems are explicitly listed. It is intended as the definitive foundational text for a new sub‑discipline at the interface of control, causality, and machine learning.

