2. Causal Stochastic Dynamics

2.1 Probabilistic setting and C‑SDEs

Let (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\ge0},\mathbb{P}) be a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions. The physical state of the system is a process x_t \in \mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^n, with \mathcal{X} open and connected. The structure of instantaneous dependencies is encoded by a directed acyclic graph G_c = (V,E) belonging to a finite set \mathcal{G} of admissible DAGs.

Definition 2.1 (C‑SDE). The process x_t follows a C‑SDE with structure G_c if

\mathrm{d}x_t = f(x_t,G_c)\,\mathrm{d}t + \sigma(x_t,G_c)\,\mathrm{d}W_t, \qquad x_0 = x^0, \tag{1}

where W_t is an m-dimensional standard Brownian motion, and the coefficients

f : \mathbb{R}^n\times\mathcal{G}\to\mathbb{R}^n,\qquad \sigma : \mathbb{R}^n\times\mathcal{G}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}

are locally Lipschitz in x uniformly in G_c and satisfy a sublinear growth condition. The DAG G_c enforces a causal factorisation: the j-th component of f and the j-th row of \sigma depend only on the parents \mathrm{Pa}_G(j) = \{ i\in V : (i\to j)\in E\}.

2.2 The do‑operator

An intervention u\in\mathcal{U} does not add a control force; it prunes the causal graph. Formally,

\mathrm{do} : \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{G},\qquad u \mapsto G'_c(u),

where G'_c(u) is obtained from the current G_c by removing a set of edges (and possibly re‑orienting them). The controlled dynamics become

\mathrm{d}x_t = f(x_t, G'_c(u))\,\mathrm{d}t + \sigma(x_t, G'_c(u))\,\mathrm{d}W_t. \tag{2}

We assume that the set of reachable graphs \mathcal{G}_{\mathrm{do}} is compact in the discrete topology and that the coefficients depend continuously on the graph structure (i.e., small changes in the adjacency matrix produce small Lipschitz perturbations of f and \sigma).

---

3. Causal Risk Functional and the Feynman–Kac Bridge

Let \mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{X} be a closed set representing catastrophic failure, assumed to be a smooth manifold with boundary. The system starts outside \mathcal{F}.

Definition 3.1 (Causal first‑passage time). For a fixed intervention u and initial condition x\notin\mathcal{F},

\tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{u} = \inf\{ t\ge0 : x_t\in\mathcal{F} \mid x_0=x,\; \text{dynamics } (2) \text{ with } \mathrm{do}(u) \}.

Definition 3.2 (Causal Risk Functional – CRF). For a discount factor \lambda>0 measuring the imminence of danger, the causal risk functional \mathcal{R}_u : \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F} \to [0,1] is

\mathcal{R}_u(x) = \mathbb{E}_x\Big[ e^{-\lambda \tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{u}} \;\Big|\; \mathrm{do}(u),\, G'_c(u) \Big], \tag{3}

with the boundary condition \mathcal{R}_u|_{\partial\mathcal{F}} = 1. The notation \mathbb{E}_x emphasises the initial state x.

Theorem 3.1 (Feynman–Kac representation). Assume that f(\cdot,G'_c(u)) and \sigma(\cdot,G'_c(u)) are smooth and bounded on \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F}, and that the domain satisfies a uniform exterior cone condition. Then \mathcal{R}_u is the unique bounded classical solution of the elliptic boundary‑value problem

\begin{cases}
\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} \mathcal{R}_u - \lambda \mathcal{R}_u = 0, & \text{in } \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F},\\[4pt]
\mathcal{R}_u = 1, & \text{on } \partial\mathcal{F},
\end{cases} \tag{4}

where \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} is the infinitesimal generator of (2):

\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)}\phi = \sum_{i=1}^n f_i(x,G'_c(u))\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x_i} + \frac12\sum_{i,j=1}^n [\sigma\sigma^\top]_{ij}(x,G'_c(u))\frac{\partial^2\phi}{\partial x_i\partial x_j}. \tag{5}

Proof. Standard Feynman–Kac formula for diffusions with absorption; the discount \lambda gives the zeroth‑order term. Uniqueness follows from the maximum principle. ∎

