4. Metabolic Constraints and the Unified Viability Functional

4.1 Admissible interventions

Every intervention consumes resources. Let \Phi(u) = [E(u), T(u), B(u), L(u)]^\top be the instantaneous consumption of Energy, Time, Bandwidth, and Lifetime. The capacities are collected in M\in\mathbb{R}^4_{>0}. The set of physically admissible interventions at state x is

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) = \big\{ u\in\mathcal{U} : \Phi_i(u) < M_i,\; i=1,\dots,4 \big\}. \tag{6}

To avoid saturation, we penalise the consumption with a logarithmic barrier

C(u) = -\sum_{i=1}^4 \log\!\Big(1 - \frac{\Phi_i(u)}{M_i}\Big), \tag{7}

which grows to +\infty as any resource approaches its limit.

4.2 Causal Control Barrier Function (CCBF) – policy‑independent definition

To avoid the logical loop of defining a barrier in terms of an optimal policy that itself depends on the barrier, we construct the barrier from the worst‑case admissible risk. Define

\rho(x,G_c) = \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \mathcal{R}_u(x). \tag{8}

\rho(x,G_c) is the maximal risk that can occur under any physically feasible intervention; it characterises the intrinsic danger of the state. The causal control barrier function is then

h(x,G_c) = \epsilon - \rho(x,G_c), \tag{9}

where \epsilon > 0 is a safety margin chosen by design. The safe region is \mathcal{S}_c = \{x\in\mathcal{X} : h(x,G_c) \ge 0\}. The barrier h is completely independent of the particular control policy; it depends only on the set \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} and the current graph G_c.

4.3 Meta‑learning stability as a log‑submartingale

The system includes a meta‑learning module that updates a parameter vector \theta_t\in\Theta. The learning is event‑driven and modelled as a jump‑diffusion:

\mathrm{d}\theta_t = a(\theta_t)\,\mathrm{d}t + b(\theta_t)\,\mathrm{d}W'_t + \int_{\mathbb{R}^k} c(\theta_{t-},z)\,\widetilde{N}(\mathrm{d}t,\mathrm{d}z), \tag{10}

where W'_t is a Brownian motion (possibly correlated with W_t), and \widetilde{N} is a compensated Poisson random measure with Lévy measure \nu(\mathrm{d}z).

Let V : \Theta \to [1,\infty) be a cognitive stability functional. To guarantee a non‑negative drift contribution directly in the log‑scale, we require that \ln V is a sub‑martingale under the meta‑learning dynamics. More precisely, using Itô’s formula for jump‑diffusions,

\mathrm{d}\ln V_t = \mathcal{A}^{\theta}_c (\ln V)_t\,\mathrm{d}t + \text{martingale},

and we impose the log‑submartingale hypothesis:

\mathcal{A}^{\theta}_c (\ln V)(\theta) \ge 0, \qquad \int_{\mathbb{R}^k} \big[ \ln V(\theta + c(\theta,z)) - \ln V(\theta) \big] \nu(\mathrm{d}z) \ge 0. \tag{11}

The first inequality controls the continuous part; the second ensures that every possible jump of the learner does not decrease \ln V in expectation. This directly bypasses the Jensen gap that would appear if we only assumed V was a sub‑martingale.

4.4 The unified viability functional

We now assemble the global viability index. Given a state x, a graph G_c, meta‑parameters \theta, and a selected intervention u, the unified viability functional is

\mathcal{V}(x,G_c,\theta,u) = h(x,G_c) \; \cdot \; V(\theta) \; \cdot \; e^{-C(u)}. \tag{12}

The safe envelope is defined by \mathcal{V} \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} > 0. Taking logarithms gives the additive form

\ln \mathcal{V} = \ln h(x,G_c) + \ln V(\theta) - C(u). \tag{13}

The key innovation is to impose a unified Hamilton–Jacobi barrier condition directly on \ln\mathcal{V}. We require that for every safe state, there exists an admissible intervention u such that the generator of the controlled joint process applied to \ln\mathcal{V} satisfies

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)}^{\mathrm{tot}} (\ln \mathcal{V})(x,G_c,\theta,u) \ge -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}\big(\ln\mathcal{V} - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}\big), \tag{14}

where \alpha_{\mathcal{V}} \in \mathcal{K} (class \mathcal{K} function: strictly increasing, \alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(0)=0), and \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)}^{\mathrm{tot}} is the total generator acting on the triple (x_t,G_c,\theta_t) with the cost C(u) considered as part of the functional (formally C(u) is not differentiated by the generator of x and \theta, but its contribution appears through the direct time derivative of the control policy; we will make this precise in the proof).

In Section 5 we will show how condition (14) almost surely keeps \ln\mathcal{V} bounded away from \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}.

