6. Regularity, Viscosity Solutions, and HJB Perspectives

The proof of Theorem 5.1 used the condition (14) as an assumption. In practice, verifying this condition requires a deeper understanding of the generators involved. The risk functional \mathcal{R}_u was introduced as a classical solution of an elliptic PDE. When coefficients are merely Lipschitz or the domain lacks smoothness, \mathcal{R}_u is interpreted in the viscosity sense. More importantly, the worst‑case risk \rho(x,G_c) = \sup_{u} \mathcal{R}_u(x) is an upper envelope of classical solutions and naturally satisfies a Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman (HJB) equation:

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \big[ \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} \rho - \lambda \rho \big] = 0, \qquad \rho|_{\partial\mathcal{F}} = 1. \tag{19}

This HJB characterization places \rho squarely in the framework of stochastic optimal control and viscosity solutions. Under suitable non‑degeneracy conditions, \rho is the unique bounded viscosity solution of (19). Consequently, the barrier h = \epsilon - \rho inherits a viscosity inequality:

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} h \ge -\lambda \rho = \lambda (h - \epsilon).

While this does not yet give the full unified condition (14), it provides a concrete expression for the barrier component. The unified condition (14) can be seen as a HJBI (Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman–Isaacs) viability inequality for the total log‑viability, where the control u attempts to maximise the drift of L to counteract the natural decay. Existence of a feasible u is equivalent to a pointwise controllability condition of the switched generator.

The viscosity viewpoint also resolves potential issues with the smoothness of the value function under graph switching. The theory of viscosity solutions for hybrid control systems is mature; our setting fits into that framework, guaranteeing that the value functions are continuous and satisfy the PDE in the viscosity sense even when classical solutions do not exist.

