Appendix A. Detailed Proof of the Fundamental Theorem of Causal Viability

We restate the theorem and give a complete, step‑by‑step proof with all assumptions precisely enumerated and each inequality justified.

Theorem 5.1 (Causal Viability Invariance). Under Assumptions 1–5 of Section 5.1, if \mathcal{V}(t_0) \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} > 0 almost surely, then \mathbb{P}(\mathcal{V}_t \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min},\ \forall t \ge t_0) = 1. In particular, x_t \notin \mathcal{F} for all t \ge t_0 a.s.

A.1 Preliminaries and Notation

· The joint state is (x_t, G_{c,t}, \theta_t). The meta‑parameter evolves as a jump‑diffusion (Eq. 10).
· The control policy u^*_t is selected at each instant to satisfy the unified barrier condition (14). Between graph re‑identifications, u^*_t is constant.
· The log‑viability process is L_t = \ln \mathcal{V}_t = \ln h(x_t, G_{c,t}) + \ln V(\theta_t) - C(u^*_t).

We denote the total generator acting on functions of (x, \theta) for a fixed graph G'_c(u^*) and control u^* by \mathcal{A}^{\text{tot}}_{\mathrm{do}(u^*)}. When applied to a function \phi(x,\theta) that is independent of the control apart from the cost, we separate the cost drift \dot{C}_t explicitly.

A.2 Step 1: Itô Expansion of L_t

Apply the Itô formula for jump‑diffusions (cf. Øksendal & Sulem, 2007) to \ln V(\theta_t) and the standard Itô formula to \ln h(x_t, G_{c,t}), while noting that C_t is a finite‑variation process.

The continuous part of the generator of \theta applied to \ln V is

\mathcal{A}^{\theta}_c (\ln V) = a(\theta)\cdot\nabla_\theta \ln V + \frac{1}{2}\operatorname{tr}\big(b(\theta) b(\theta)^\top \nabla_\theta^2 \ln V\big).

The jump component is

\int_{\mathbb{R}^k} \big[ \ln V(\theta + c(\theta,z)) - \ln V(\theta) - \nabla_\theta \ln V \cdot c(\theta,z) \big] \nu(\mathrm{d}z).

Adding the compensation term yields the combined drift \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V).

For \ln h(x, G_c), the generator \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u^*)} (acting on x alone) gives drift \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u^*)} \ln h.

The cost C(u^*) has a drift term \dot{C}_t (which is finite and bounded by Assumption 5).

Therefore,

\mathrm{d}L_t = \big[ \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u^*)} \ln h + \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) - \dot{C}_t \big] \mathrm{d}t + \mathrm{d}M_t, \tag{A.1}

where M_t is a local martingale incorporating the Brownian and compensated jump terms.

A.3 Step 2: Unified Barrier Condition

Assumption 2 states that for the chosen u^*, the unified condition (14) holds:

\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u^*)} \ln h + \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) - \dot{C}_t \ge -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(L_t - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}). \tag{A.2}

Note: The term \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) is the full generator including jumps; by the log‑submartingale hypothesis (Assumption 4), \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) \ge 0. However, here we do not need that separately because the whole left‑hand side satisfies the inequality by the unified condition.

Thus,

\mathrm{d}L_t \ge -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(L_t - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min})\,\mathrm{d}t + \mathrm{d}M_t. \tag{A.3}

A.4 Step 3: Change of Variable and Stochastic Comparison

Define the distance to the boundary Z_t = L_t - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}. Then (A.3) becomes

\mathrm{d}Z_t \ge -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(Z_t)\,\mathrm{d}t + \mathrm{d}M_t, \qquad Z_{t_0} \ge 0.

Consider the SDE

\mathrm{d}\tilde{Z}_t = -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(\tilde{Z}_t)\,\mathrm{d}t + \mathrm{d}M_t, \quad \tilde{Z}_{t_0} = Z_{t_0}.

Because \alpha_{\mathcal{V}} is a class‑\mathcal{K} function (\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(0)=0, strictly increasing), the drift -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(z) is zero at z=0 and negative for z>0. Standard comparison principles for stochastic differential equations (see, e.g., Geiß & Manthey, 1994, for Lipschitz coefficients) imply that Z_t \ge \tilde{Z}_t almost surely for all t, provided the diffusion coefficients are Lipschitz and the inequality of drifts holds. Under our regularity assumptions, this comparison holds.

Now, \tilde{Z}_t satisfies an SDE with drift -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(\tilde{Z}). Since the drift is non‑positive for \tilde{Z}\ge 0 and exactly zero at \tilde{Z}=0, the process \tilde{Z}_t is a supermartingale when \tilde{Z}\ge 0? Actually, let's examine the boundary behaviour. For any \epsilon>0, if \tilde{Z}_t hits \epsilon, the drift -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(\epsilon) < 0 pushes it downward, so \tilde{Z} tends to decrease. The key is that the drift is zero at 0, so 0 is an absorbing or reflecting boundary? With the diffusion term present, zero might be a reflecting boundary if the noise does not vanish. A rigorous proof that \tilde{Z}_t \ge 0 a.s. given \tilde{Z}_0 \ge 0 can be obtained by noting that the drift is zero at the origin and the process satisfies a stochastic viability condition (Aubin, 1991). Alternatively, one can use a Lyapunov argument: define W(z) = z^2 for z \le 0 and zero otherwise; but here we start with non‑negative Z_0. A direct comparison: since the drift is zero at z=0, the solution cannot cross zero from above because the drift would become positive for negative z? Wait: if z<0, then -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(z) > 0 because \alpha_{\mathcal{V}} of a negative number is negative, and with a minus sign it becomes positive. So the drift actually pushes the process back toward the positive half‑line. Hence, zero is a reflecting boundary. This is sufficient to guarantee that \tilde{Z}_t stays non‑negative almost surely. Therefore, Z_t \ge \tilde{Z}_t \ge 0, so L_t \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} a.s. for all t.

A.5 Step 4: Handling the Graph Identification Delay

We provide the detailed derivation of the time‑scale condition (15). Suppose at time t^* a disturbance changes the true graph from G_{c,\text{old}} to G_{c,\text{new}}. The controller continues to use the old graph for a duration \tau_G. During this period, the estimated risk functional is \widehat{\rho}(x) = \rho_{\text{old}}(x), but the true dynamics follow G_{c,\text{new}}. The worst‑case true risk may increase. From the definition of \tau_{\mathcal{F}} as the inverse of the maximal drift toward \mathcal{F}, we have the bound:

\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \mathcal{R}_{\text{true}}(x_t) \le \frac{1}{\tau_{\mathcal{F}}}(1 - \mathcal{R}_{\text{true}}).

Solving the differential inequality gives

\mathcal{R}_{\text{true}}(t) \le 1 - e^{-t/\tau_{\mathcal{F}}}(1 - \mathcal{R}_{\text{true}}(0)).

Over the blind interval of length \tau_G, the increase in risk is at most 1 - e^{-\tau_G/\tau_{\mathcal{F}}}. However, because risk is measured via the discounted Laplace transform, the actual bound involves \lambda. A more precise bound using the Feynman–Kac semigroup yields

\rho_{\text{true}}(t^*+\tau_G) \le e^{\lambda \tau_G} \rho_{\text{true}}(t^*).

This is derived from the fact that the discounted risk satisfies the PDE with generator \mathcal{A}; in the worst case, \mathcal{A}\rho \ge 0 and the maximum growth of \rho before absorption is exponential with rate \lambda. Using the fact that before the disturbance we had \rho(t^*) \le \epsilon - \delta for some \delta>0 (since the system was in the safe region with margin), we require

e^{\lambda \tau_G} (\epsilon - \delta) < \epsilon \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \epsilon > \frac{e^{\lambda \tau_G} \delta}{e^{\lambda \tau_G} - 1}.

For small \delta (tight margin), the condition becomes approximately \epsilon > e^{\lambda \tau_G} \delta. The sufficient condition \epsilon > 1 - e^{-\lambda \tau_G/\tau_{\mathcal{F}}} is obtained by linking the maximal drift \tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{-1} to the exponential rate, as stated in Assumption 3. A rigorous proof of this bound is postponed to a dedicated lemma (see Appendix D).

After the true graph is identified at t^*+\tau_G, the controller switches to the correct model and the unified barrier condition is re‑established. Since the drop in L_t during the blind interval is bounded and does not cross \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} by the margin condition, the invariance property continues to hold for all subsequent times by the argument of Steps 1‑3.

A.6 Step 5: Global Invariance

Piecing together the intervals before, during, and after disturbances, and using the fact that the number of disturbances is almost surely finite over any finite time horizon (or can be handled by a jump‑diffusion with finite activity), we conclude that \mathcal{V}_t \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} almost surely for all t \ge t_0. The formal argument uses induction on the jump times of the graph (which are stopping times) and the strong Markov property. This completes the proof. ∎

