Appendix C. Viscosity Solutions and the HJB Characterization of the Risk Functional

We provide the rigorous viscosity framework for the worst‑case risk \rho(x, G_c).

C.1 The HJB Equation for \rho

Recall \rho(x,G_c) = \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \mathcal{R}_u(x). For each fixed u, \mathcal{R}_u solves the linear elliptic PDE (4). Standard results from optimal control (e.g., Fleming & Soner, 2006) show that the value function of a discounted optimal control problem, which \rho resembles, satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation:

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \big[ \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} \rho(x) - \lambda \rho(x) \big] = 0, \qquad \rho|_{\partial\mathcal{F}} = 1. \tag{C.1}

Because the control set is finite (the set of reachable graphs is finite), this is a finite‑state switching HJB equation.

Definition C.1 (Viscosity solution). A bounded, continuous function \rho on \overline{\mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F}} is a viscosity subsolution (resp. supersolution) of (C.1) if for any smooth test function \phi touching \rho from above (resp. below) at a point x_0, the inequality holds with \le 0 (resp. \ge 0). It is a viscosity solution if it is both.

Theorem C.1. Assume the coefficients are Lipschitz and the domain satisfies an exterior sphere condition. Then \rho is the unique bounded, continuous viscosity solution of (C.1).

Proof sketch. The proof follows the standard Perron’s method and comparison principle for HJB equations with discount (see Crandall et al., 1992). Uniqueness holds because the Hamiltonian is proper (due to the discount term -\lambda \rho). Existence can be shown by showing that the supremum of the classical solutions (which are viscosity sub‑ and supersolutions) is itself a viscosity solution under the finite control set.

C.2 Viscosity Interpretation of the Unified Barrier Condition

The barrier h = \epsilon - \rho also satisfies a viscosity inequality. From (C.1), we have

\sup_{u} \big[ \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} h + \lambda \rho \big] = 0 \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \sup_{u} \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} h = -\lambda \rho = \lambda(h - \epsilon). \tag{C.2}

This provides a concrete bound on the available drift. The unified condition (14) then becomes a viscosity inequality for the log‑viability L:

\sup_{u} \big[ \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)}(\ln h) + \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) - \dot{C}(u) \big] \ge -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(L - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}).

Because \ln h is a smooth function of h (away from zero), this can be expressed directly in terms of the generators. The viscosity formulation ensures that even when classical derivatives do not exist, the inequality holds in the appropriate weak sense, and the comparison theorem in Appendix A remains valid.

