Appendix E. Proactive Causal Topology Design – Mathematical Formulation

We show how the controller can be extended to actively reduce graph complexity to shorten \tau_G.

E.1 Graph Complexity Measure

Let \gamma(G) be a real‑valued function measuring the complexity of a DAG, e.g., number of edges, maximum in‑degree, or the sample complexity of a causal discovery algorithm. We assume \gamma(G) is finite and that removing edges reduces \gamma.

E.2 Extended Metabolic Cost

Define the augmented cost

C_{\text{aug}}(u) = C(u) + \mu \gamma(G'_c(u)), \qquad \mu > 0.

The constant \mu trades off resource consumption against structural simplicity.

E.3 Augmented Viability Functional and Unified Condition

The viability functional becomes

\mathcal{V}_{\text{aug}} = h \cdot V \cdot e^{-C_{\text{aug}}(u)}.

The log‑viability is L_{\text{aug}} = \ln h + \ln V - C(u) - \mu \gamma(G'_c(u)). The unified barrier condition (14) now reads

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \big[ \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{do}(u)} \ln h + \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) - \dot{C}(u) \big] \ge -\alpha_{\mathcal{V}}(L_{\text{aug}} - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}).

Because -\mu \gamma adds a constant offset (independent of x and \theta), it does not affect the generator, but it shifts the boundary. More precisely, the condition must hold with the new L_{\text{aug}}. This incentivizes the controller to select interventions that yield a sparser graph, because they increase L_{\text{aug}} (since -\mu \gamma is larger, i.e., less penalty). Consequently, the feasible control set is biased toward simple topologies.

E.4 Effect on \tau_G and the Safety Margin

By keeping the graph sparse, the identification delay \tau_G is reduced. This relaxes the requirement (15) on \epsilon, allowing a smaller safety margin and thus larger safe region. The controller thus anticipates future uncertainties and prepares for them by structural simplification. This self‑aware loop is a distinctive feature of the causal intervention framework.

