Appendix F. Summary of Critical Advice and Integration

Throughout the development of this paper, we received several key pieces of critical feedback that have been fully incorporated:

1. Circularity in the CCBF definition. Advice: Define h independently of u^* using the worst‑case admissible risk \rho. Integration: Section 4.2 constructs h = \epsilon - \rho(x,G_c) purely from the set \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} and the current graph, eliminating any recursive dependence on the policy.
2. Jensen gap in jump‑diffusions. Advice: The sub‑martingale property of V does not carry to \ln V; switch to log‑submartingale. Integration: Assumption 4 now directly imposes \mathcal{A}^{\theta}(\ln V) \ge 0 and the corresponding jump condition (Appendix B).
3. Boundary drift sign and Itô correction. Advice: The term -\alpha(h)/h is negative for class‑\mathcal{K} \alpha; the quadratic variation term \|\nabla h^\top\sigma\|^2/h^2 can blow up. Integration: We applied the barrier condition directly to the unified log‑viability L_t, so the drift is evaluated at L, not at h=0. Because the boundary is at L = \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}>0, h is bounded away from zero, avoiding the quadratic variation explosion. The class‑\mathcal{K} condition on \alpha_{\mathcal{V}} ensures repulsion at the boundary (Appendix A).
4. Switched generators and viscosity solutions. Advice: The actual system is a switched diffusion; \rho satisfies an HJB equation as a viscosity solution. Integration: Section 6 and Appendix C provide the HJB characterization and explain how the viscosity framework supports the unified barrier condition.
5. Proactive topology design. Advice: The interaction between \tau_G and graph density can be exploited. Integration: Appendix E formalizes the augmented cost and shows how the controller can proactively simplify the causal structure to reduce identification delays.

These refinements ensure that the ACRL v2 framework stands on a mathematically rigorous foundation, ready for further development and practical implementation.


